  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Congoville is showing early but meaningful reach as an open-access scholarly book in the humanities and art, with 790 recorded downloads and at least one documented teaching-related appearance in a Middelheim Museum book-launch video. Its record also points to relevance for sustainability and policy discussions through an SDG alignment with Sustainable Cities and Communities, while citation impact remains at zero so far.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Congoville is a scholarly book that translates colonial history into a public-facing cultural and spatial inquiry, asking how the traces of Belgium\u2019s colonial past remain visible in contemporary urban and museum landscapes. The record shows that this framing has found an audience: the book has been downloaded 790 times through OAPEN, indicating active use rather than only formal catalog presence.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest additional signal is educational and interpretive uptake. A Middelheim Museum book-launch video explicitly discusses the book, suggesting that it is being used in a curated public-learning context rather than circulating only within academic libraries. That kind of use fits the book\u2019s interdisciplinary profile, which the metadata places in the humanities and art.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The available indicators also point to broader civic relevance. The book is associated with the Sustainable Cities and Communities SDG, which aligns with its attention to urban space, memory, and the material afterlives of colonialism. This does not by itself demonstrate policy influence, but it does show that the work has been read as relevant to questions of how cities represent, preserve, and confront difficult histories.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the citation record is still quiet, with zero citations reported in OpenAlex. That means the present story is one of early dissemination and contextual uptake rather than established scholarly citation impact. The evidence available here supports a narrative of public accessibility, teaching and museum-adjacent use, and conceptual relevance, while leaving longer-term scholarly influence to develop over time.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
}